From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: USA-RedDragon <USA-RedDragon@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 19:32:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(delegate): a subagent's own subagents run in the
 background

A subagent that called delegate_task ran its children inside that tool
call: the call joined them, so the subagent sat in one tool call for as
long as its slowest child ran (two hours and more), and a steer or an
agent_send to it was read only when the call returned.

Each child of a subagent now runs as a background unit under it, as a
top-level chat's do. delegate_task returns its ids at once. When the
subagent ends its turn while a child runs, its own unit stays open and it
shows as waiting, with waiting_on naming the children. Each child's result
wakes it as its next input, in the top-level completion format, and a
steer or agent_send wakes it at once. It finishes, and its parent gets its
result, only when it ends a turn with no child running and no watched job.
A subagent that ends without waiting (stopped, or its run failed) stops
its running children and lists their statuses and partial summaries under
children on its result; children are never handed to the grandparent,
which did not brief them. A restart handoff carries the running children
with the subagent: the next gateway resumes them from their stored
sessions under the resumed subagent, which goes back to waiting.

A subagent's process_manage wait returns within 120 seconds and is
released at once by a message to it or a finished child of its own; the
released job is watched as handoff resume_on_exit does. The depth limit is
unchanged.
